coder › Done on branch `work/json-flag` (3 commits on top of `01e6660`).

What changed:
- `src/csv.ts` (new): exports `parseCsv(text: string): string[][]`. Splits on `\n`, strips one trailing `\r` per line (CRLF support), ignores a single trailing newline, and parses each line with a small quoted-field state machine: a field starting with `"` runs quoted, `""` inside becomes one `"`, commas inside quotes don't split.
- `src/cli.ts`: added `--json` handling to `run()`. First parsed row is the header; data rows become objects via `Object.fromEntries` (all values stay strings), printed as `JSON.stringify(objs) + "\n"`. Empty input or header-only prints `[]\n`. A data row whose field count differs from the header returns code 1 with one line, e.g. `error: line 2: expected 2 fields but got 1\n`, and no JSON. Usage line is now `usage: csvtool --upper | --count | --json`.
- Tests: new `src/csv.test.ts` (plain split, empty input, quoted comma, doubled quote, CRLF); extended `src/cli.test.ts` (usage mentions `--json`, basic conversion, string values, empty/header-only `[]`, quotes+CRLF through `--json`, ragged row names line 2 with code 1 and trailing newline).

Decisions the task did not pin down:
- Error wording: the spec only requires code 1 and one line naming the 1-based line number with no JSON, so I used `error: line N: expected H fields but got G\n`.

